\pard\sb280\sa100\b\fs28 Time options\par

\pard\sb160\sa60\b\fs24 Hours\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
\b hh\b0\tab Hour in 12-hour format, with a leading zero\par
\b h\b0\tab Hour in 12-hour format\par
\b HH\b0\tab Hour in 24-hour format, with a leading zero\par
\b H\b0\tab Hour in 24-hour format\par
\b w\b0\'b1\b offset\b0\tab Other time zone, 12-hour hour, local time plus or minus the offset\par
\b W\b0\'b1\b offset\b0\tab Other time zone, 24-hour hour\par

The offset is whole hours (\b W+13\b0), hours and minutes (\b W+5:30\b0, \b W-3:30\b0), or decimal hours (\b W+5.5\b0 is 5 hours 30 minutes, \b W+5.75\b0 is 5 hours 45 minutes). \b W+5.30\b0 is 5.30 hours, which is 18 minutes. Use \b W+5:30\b0 or \b W+5.5\b0 for half an hour.\par

The code prints that zone's hour. Date and time codes written after it (\b nn\b0, \b ss\b0, \b dd\b0, \b mm\b0, \b yyyy\b0, \b tt\b0, \b dddd\b0, and so on) stay in that zone, so the date changes when the offset crosses midnight. The next \b W\b0 or \b w\b0 offset starts again from local time.\par

\b W+5:30:"yyyy-mm-dd HH:nn"\b0 formats only the quoted text in that zone. Use it for a second clock, including a 30-minute or 45-minute zone such as India, Newfoundland, or Nepal.\par

From UTC-5, \b w+05\b0 is UTC and \b W+10:30:"HH:nn"\b0 is India. \b W+13:"yyyy-mm-dd HH:nn"\b0 is China from US Eastern standard time, and the date moves ahead after 23:00. From UTC+2, \b w-02\b0 is UTC. \b W+5:30:nn\b0 prints the zone hour, then that zone's minutes.\par

