\pard\sb280\sa100\b\fs28 Other options\par

\pard\sb160\sa60\b\fs24 Swatch Internet Time (.beat time)\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
A decimal time from 1998, marketed by Swatch so people in different time zones could name a time without using zones. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swatch_Internet_Time"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1 Wikipedia: Swatch Internet Time}}}\par
\b @@@\b0\tab Beat time\par
\b @@@.@\b0\tab Beat time with one decimal place\par
\b @@@.@@\b0\tab Beat time with two decimal places\par

\pard\sb160\sa60\b\fs24 Formatting\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
\b \\n\b0\tab Line break\par
\b "..."\b0\tab Quotes. Text inside quotes is shown as written and is not read as a format code.\par
\b LDATE\b0\tab Windows long date\par
\b DATE\b0\tab Windows short date\par
\b TIME\b0\tab Windows time\par
\b JD\b0\tab Julian date, used in astronomy\par
\b OD\b0\tab Ordinal date (YYYY-DDD) in UTC\par
\b Od\b0\tab Ordinal date (YYYY-DDD) in local time\par
\b POSIX\b0\tab POSIX time, seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC\par

\pard\sb160\sa60\b\fs24 System uptime\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
\b Sd\b0\tab Days the system has been running. Do not combine with \b Sa\b0.\par
\b Sa\b0\tab Total hours the system has been running. Do not combine with \b Sd\b0.\par
\b Sh\b0\tab Hours within the current day. Do not combine with \b Sa\b0.\par
\b Sn\b0\tab Minutes\par
\b Ss\b0\tab Seconds\par
\b ST\b0\tab Short uptime, h:mm:ss\par
On Windows versions before Vista this uses GetTickCount(), so uptime longer than 49.7 days wraps to 0. Vista and later do not have that limit.\par
Uptime codes also accept padding. \b S__a\b0 pads with up to two spaces when the hours are below 100. \b S___dd\b0 pads with up to three spaces and keeps at least two digits, so three hours is shown as "   03".\par

\pard\sb280\sa100\b\fs28 Command line\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
\b /exit\b0\tab Exit T-Clock\par
\b /prop\b0\tab Open T-Clock properties\par
\b /start\b0\tab Start the stopwatch, opening it if needed\par
\b /stop\b0\tab Pause the stopwatch\par
\b /reset\b0\tab Reset the stopwatch to 0. This does not stop it.\par
\b /lap\b0\tab Record the current lap\par
\b /sync\b0\tab Synchronize the clock. Windows asks for administrator approval.\par
\b /syncopt\b0\tab Open the time-sync options, which can also sync now\par
\b /exit-explorer\b0\tab Ask Explorer to exit, and end it after 15 seconds if it is still running\par
\b /restart-explorer\b0\tab Exit Explorer, then start it again\par
\b /SEH /exit\b0\tab Unload the exception handler and exit\par

\pard\sb280\sa100\b\fs28 Hotkeys\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
T-Clock can assign hotkeys to:\par
Open the stopwatch.\par
Open add and edit timers.\par
Open the properties window.\par
Show the T-Clock calendar.\par
Open the timer watch window.\par
The timer watch closes itself when no timer is being watched.\par

\pard\sb280\sa100\b\fs28 PC beep tones (.pcb)\par
\pard\sa40\b0\fs22
T-Clock can play tones through the PC speaker with .pcb files. Use these when there is no sound card, or when you want these tones. A .pcb file is plain text. Each line is a duration and a frequency. Edit it in any text editor, such as Notepad.\par
One demo.pcb file is in the Waves folder. Its lines mean:\par
\b 100, 400\b0\tab A 400 Hz tone for 100 ms\par
\b 100, -1\b0\tab Silence for 100 ms\par
\b 100, 500\b0\tab A 500 Hz tone for 100 ms\par
\b 100, -1\b0\tab Silence for 100 ms\par
\b 100, 600\b0\tab A 600 Hz tone for 100 ms\par
\b 100, -1\b0\tab Silence for 100 ms\par
\b 100, 500\b0\tab A 500 Hz tone for 100 ms\par
\b 100, -1\b0\tab Silence for 100 ms\par
\b 100, 400\b0\tab A 400 Hz tone for 100 ms\par
\b 500, -1\b0\tab Silence for 500 ms at the end, so a loop does not click\par
The first number is the duration in milliseconds. The second number is the pitch in hertz. A pitch of -1 is a pause. Allowed pitches are 37 to 32767.\par
